00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
YUFENG GUO: Sometimes
there's a misconception

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.248
that Google Cloud
only runs TensorFlow.

00:00:04.248 --> 00:00:06.540
Well, there are many deep
learning libraries out there.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.960
And GCP runs much more
than just TensorFlow.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.460
How do we get other
machine learning

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.350
libraries running on GCP?

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:14.760
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:18.688 --> 00:00:19.670
[DIGITAL BLIP]

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:21.143
[BEEP]

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.380
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.110
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:30.420
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.759
And on this episode
we're going to see how

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:35.580
to run PyTorch on Google Cloud.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:36.940
There's a lot of options.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.910
So let's dive right in.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.890
If you want to get
your hands on PyTorch

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.350
and have no
installation or setup,

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.940
then there's,
well, no easier way

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.630
to do so than using something
like Colab or Kaggle kernels.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.640
You can just sign in
with your Google account,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.320
and you're ready to use PyTorch.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.160
Just import and go.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.230
Colab has a neat GitHub
integration additionally

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.620
that allows you to pull in any
public IPython notebook file

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.230
on GitHub directly into Colab.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.850
So if you're working
through an examples repo,

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:08.610
and you want to try
modifying and running

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.590
the code for yourself,
it's super easy.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.420
You can just go to File, Open
Notebook, select the GitHub

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:21.940
tab, and paste in the URL of the
repository or that user's name.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.490
You can also link directly
to a GitHub notebook in Colab

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.270
by copying the URL that
comes after GitHub.com,

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:33.610
and pasting it directly
after Colab.research.g

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.950
oogle.com/GitHub, which
we are showing here.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.270
Now, on the Kaggle
side of things,

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.090
there is a vast
library of kernels

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.730
available from the
community across many data

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:48.090
sets, often with excellent
discussion and annotations.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.202
So you don't have to
start from scratch.

00:01:50.202 --> 00:01:51.660
And, again, you
don't have to worry

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.420
about installing anything
or doing configuration.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.750
Just sign in, and you're
ready to edit your code.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.540
Another option for
the PyTorch developers

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.400
out there are the deep
learning virtual machines,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.120
which we've covered a couple
different ways on this show.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.060
You can select an image that
is pre baked with PyTorch,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.600
alongside any Nvidia drivers
you may need for GPUs

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.790
that you might choose to attach.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.650
This option is
great if you really

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.570
want to crank up
that compute power

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.130
and have full control
over your environment,

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.520
including being able to
save your work directly

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.440
to your virtual
machine, as opposed

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.340
to Google Drive on Colab
or onto Kaggle kernels.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.320
Finally, I want to
point out that Google

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.430
is working with the PyTorch team
to enable TensorBoard support

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:40.600
to visualize training progress,
as well as TPU support.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:45.030
So considering that you can
run Colab with TPUs for free,

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.370
that's going to be
an awesome combo.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.400
So if you're overflowing with
ideas about how you'd like

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.150
to use Cloud TPUs with
PyTorch, email the team

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:59.400
at PyTorch-TPU@googlegroups.com
to tell them what types

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.340
of PyTorch workloads you
would most be interested

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:03.990
in accelerating.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:07.170
There are options to suit all
kinds of PyTorch use cases

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:08.460
on GCP.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.070
Whether you are looking
to just get started,

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.980
or if you're looking to
spin up a big training job,

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.410
there's a solid
PyTorch friendly way

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.110
to get it done on Google Cloud.

